.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt EVENTFD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm eventfd ,
.Nm eventfd_read ,
.Nm eventfd_write
.Nd create a file descriptor for event notification
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/eventfd.h
.Ft int
.Fn eventfd "unsigned int initval" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn eventfd_read "int fd" "eventfd_t *value"
.Ft int
.Fn eventfd_write "int fd" "eventfd_t value"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn eventfd
creates a file descriptor that refers to a 64-bit unsigned counter, set to
.Fa initval .
The descriptor can be inherited, duplicated and passed to other processes
with
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
messages
.Pq see Xr unix 4 ;
every copy refers to the same counter.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument is zero or a bitwise OR of:
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv EFD_SEMAPHORE"
.It Dv EFD_CLOEXEC
Set the close-on-exec flag on the new descriptor.
.It Dv EFD_NONBLOCK
Set
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
on the descriptor.
.It Dv EFD_SEMAPHORE
Read the counter as a semaphore (see below).
.El
.Pp
A
.Xr write 2
of an 8-byte value adds it to the counter.
The largest value the counter holds is 0xfffffffffffffffe.
If adding the value would exceed it, the write blocks until a read
makes room, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
on a non-blocking descriptor.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
of at least 8 bytes waits until the counter is not zero.
It then returns the counter's value and sets it to zero or, with
.Dv EFD_SEMAPHORE ,
returns 1 and decrements the counter by 1.
A non-blocking read of a zero counter fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
.Pp
The descriptor is readable for
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2
and
.Xr kqueue 2
.Pq Dv EVFILT_READ
while the counter is not zero, and writable
.Pq Dv EVFILT_WRITE
while it is below its largest value.
.Pp
.Fn eventfd_read
and
.Fn eventfd_write
read and write the counter through
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn eventfd
returns a new file descriptor, or \-1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Fn eventfd_read
and
.Fn eventfd_write
return 0, or \-1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn eventfd
fails if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
contains an unknown flag.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptors.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
The system file table is full.
.El
.Pp
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
on the descriptor fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The buffer is smaller than 8 bytes, or a write's value is
0xffffffffffffffff.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The descriptor is non-blocking and the operation would block.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The buffer is outside the process's address space.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal interrupted a blocking operation.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr pipe 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr unix 4
.Sh STANDARDS
.Fn eventfd
is compatible with the Linux and
.Fx
system calls of the same name.
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn eventfd
first appeared in FoxynBSD.
